 "timeline": {
  "current_model": "GAPDH is a tetrameric glycolytic enzyme that catalyzes the oxidative phosphorylation of glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate via a conserved active-site cysteine (Cys152 in human); this cysteine acts as a versatile redox switch\u2014subject to S-nitrosylation (by NO/iNOS/nNOS), S-glutathionylation (by H2O2), succination (by fumarate/DMF), alkylation (by itaconate), and reversible oxidation (by H2O2)\u2014that inactivates glycolysis, re-routes carbon to the pentose phosphate pathway, and enables GAPDH to adopt multiple non-glycolytic functions including: (1) binding Siah1 and translocating to the nucleus where it is acetylated by p300/CBP (at Lys160), trans-nitrosylates nuclear proteins (SIRT1, HDAC2, DNA-PK), and activates p53-dependent apoptosis; (2) acting as a heme chaperone delivering mitochondrially-generated heme to apo-sGC, IDO1, TDO, myoglobin, and hemoglobins; (3) binding TERC to inhibit telomerase; (4) associating with NAMPT to enable its nuclear translocation for NAD+ salvage; (5) translocating to the nucleus to facilitate DNA base excision repair via DNA Pol \u03b2; (6) appearing on the cell surface to function as a transferrin/lactoferrin/plasminogen receptor for iron homeostasis; and (7) being post-translationally modified by CARM1 methylation (Arg234) and VRK2/FBXW10-dependent ubiquitination (Ser151) to regulate glycolysis and NF-\u03baB signaling in cancer."
 },
